// A fleet use case: the ordered hops that fulfil one business capability.
//
// This file is the whole shape of the axis. A `dynamic view` tagged
// `#cap-<slug>` opts in to grading; an untagged one is somebody's hand-drawn
// diagram and loam never says a word about it. The slug is the capability id
// from architecture/capabilities.yaml with every `/` flattened to `-`, because
// a LikeC4 tag name cannot carry a slash — `identity/tokens` would be
// `#cap-identity-tokens`.
//
// It lives in its own file rather than in architecture/landscape.likec4 for a
// reason that is about people, not parsing: the map belongs to whoever owns the
// fleet's topology, and a flow changes when a business need changes. Different
// reasons to edit are different files. The whole `architecture/` directory is
// read as ONE LikeC4 project, so the steps below resolve against the landscape's
// elements even though this file declares none — which also means exactly one
// file in this directory declares the `specification` block, and it is not this
// one.
//
// THE TAG MUST BE THE FIRST THING IN THE VIEW BODY. Written after `title` it is
// five parse errors, none of which mentions tags. That is the mistake every
// author makes once.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Two things about this particular flow are worth reading before you copy it.
//
// The first hop is a PERSON, and it is here on purpose: `customer ->
// checkoutWeb` is backed by a real edge that carries no `metadata { op }`, and
// checkout-web is a real services/ directory — so every condition of
// `usecase.step-unlinked` is met except the one that matters, which is that a
// person is not a caller. Somebody clicking a button owes no operationId. Delete
// that exemption from the checker and this file is what notices: the example
// fleet's warning count moves from eight to nine and test/examples.test.ts
// fails.
//
// The hop from checkout-web to order-service is DELIBERATELY NOT DRAWN, and its
// absence is the more useful half of the lesson. The landscape declares two
// edges for that pair — `Calls createOrder` and `Calls getOrder` — so a step
// between those two elements names two operations, and loam refuses to guess
// which one the flow exercises: `usecase.step-contested` (warn), listing both
// candidates. That is the check working. The two fixes are the ones the message
// offers: draw the hop between elements that name exactly one operation, or give
// the candidates one operation between them.

views {
  dynamic view uc_order_notification {
    #cap-order-notifications
    #req-NOTIFY-ONCE
    title 'Order notification'
    description 'A placed order reaches the customer as exactly one confirmation'

    // A person, not a caller — see above.
    customer -> marketplace.checkoutWeb 'places the order'

    // Producer to topic: the edge carries `publishes`, so the hop reaches a
    // declared API — an AsyncAPI one — and loam does not also demand an HTTP
    // operationId for it. The event spine grades this edge on its own terms.
    marketplace.orderService -> kafka.orderEvents 'announces OrderPlaced'

    // Topic to consumer, the same exemption from the other side. Modelling the
    // TOPIC rather than the broker is what keeps this readable: an edge into
    // `kafka` itself would put every service in the fleet on one node.
    kafka.orderEvents -> marketplace.notificationService 'sends the confirmation'
  }
}
